The most suspicious buggy method is:

`com.google.javascript.jscomp.NameAnalyzer.createNameInformation(com.google.javascript.jscomp.NodeTraversal, com.google.javascript.rhino.Node, com.google.javascript.rhino.Node)`

Why:
- The failure is triggered on `goog.inherits(ns.PageSelectionModel.ChangeEvent, ns.PageSelectionModel.FooEvent);`
- The internal error says: `Unexpected variable goog`
- In `NameAnalyzer.FindReferences.visit(...)`, every `NAME` node and certain qualified names are passed to `createNameInformation(...)`.
- In `createNameInformation(...)`, this block is too permissive:

```java
if (parent.isCall() && t.inGlobalScope()) {
  CodingConvention convention = compiler.getCodingConvention();
  SubclassRelationship classes = convention.getClassesDefinedByCall(parent);
  if (classes != null) {
    NameInformation nameInfo = new NameInformation();
    nameInfo.name = classes.subclassName;
    nameInfo.onlyAffectsClassDef = true;
    nameInfo.superclass = classes.superclassName;
    return nameInfo;
  }
}
```

It checks only `parent.isCall()`, not whether the current node `n` is actually the callee of that call. So when traversal visits the inner `NAME goog` inside the callee `goog.inherits`, it can incorrectly treat that `goog` node as if it were the whole class-defining call target. Then `FindReferences.visit(...)` uses `n.getQualifiedName()` on that node, which becomes `"goog"`, and records a reference involving `"goog"` in the class-definition path. That matches the crash symptom exactly: `Unexpected variable goog`.

This also explains the second failing test: the pass mishandles the inheritance call and leaves code behind instead of removing it consistently.

Dynamic verification was attempted but came back inconclusive due to debugger session loss, so the diagnosis is based on strong static evidence.

Best fix location:
- Restrict the class-defining-call logic in `createNameInformation(...)` so it only applies when `n` is the actual callee node of the `CALL` (for example `parent.getFirstChild() == n`), not arbitrary descendants like the `NAME goog` inside `goog.inherits`.

